## DBsubject(Mechanics)
## DBchapter(Oscillatory Motion and Waves)
## DBsection(Hooke's Law: Stress and Strain Revisited)
## Date(December 2017)
## Institution(Brock University)
## Author(Brandon Lostracco)
## Edited (Connor Wilson, May 1, 2018)
## TitleText1('College Physics')
## AuthorText1('Urone et. al')
## EditionText1('2017')
## Section1('16.1')
## Problem1('6')
## KEYWORDS(equilibrium, force, Hooke's, law, Newton,spring, weight)

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context('Numeric');

$length1 = random(0.190, 0.210, 0.001);
$mass1   = random(0.290, 0.310, 0.001);
$length2 = random(0.700, 0.800, 0.01);
$mass2   = random(1.90,  2.10,  0.01);
$x       = random(0.11,  0.13,  0.001);
$A1      = ($mass2 - $mass1) * (9.80) / ($length2 - $length1);
$A2      = ($length2) - (($mass2) * (9.8)) / ($A1);

BEGIN_PGML
A spring has a length of [`[$length1]`] [`\textrm{m}`] when a [`[$mass1]`] [`\textrm{kg}`] mass hangs from it, and a length of [`[$length2]`] [`\textrm{m}`] when a [`[$mass2]`] [`\textrm{kg}`] mass hangs from it.

a)  What is the force constant of the spring? 

    [_]{$A1} [`\textrm{N/m}`]

b)  What is the unloaded length of the spring?

    [_]{$A2} [`\textrm{m}`]

END_PGML
BEGIN_PGML_HINT
a) The hanging object will have extended the spring by its equilibrium distance when the object's weight and the spring force balance.

b) Recall that the spring constant is the slope of the line passing through several [`\textrm{(x, F)}`] data points, where [`\textrm{F}`] is the force applied and [`\textrm{x}`] is the distance extended. Can you find the [`y`]-intercept of this equation?
END_PGML_HINT

Context()->normalStrings;

ANS(num_cmp("$A2"));

ENDDOCUMENT();
